// HARD Question Bank - Domain 5.0 Security Program Management and Oversight (100)
window.HARD_BANK = (window.HARD_BANK || []).concat([
// ---------- 5.1 (20) ----------
{o:"5.1",q:"An 'Information Security Policy' says data must be protected according to its classification. A second document requires AES-256 for Confidential data at rest. A third gives step-by-step BitLocker setup instructions. Which classification is correct for the three documents, in order?",c:["Policy, guideline, procedure","Policy, standard, procedure","Standard, procedure, policy","Guideline, standard, procedure"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. The first and third labels fit, but a guideline is advisory. The second document says AES-256 is required for Confidential data, which makes it a mandatory standard. A guideline would be right only if it merely recommended AES-256.","Correct. The first document states management intent (policy), the second sets a mandatory, specific technical requirement (standard), and the third gives step-by-step BitLocker instructions (procedure).","Incorrect. The document saying data must be protected per classification is high-level intent, which is a policy, not a standard. This order would fit only if the first document listed concrete mandatory settings and the last stated broad intent.","Incorrect. The first document is a broad statement of intent that applies to everyone, so it is a policy, not optional advice. A guideline-first order would fit only if the opening document merely offered recommendations."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A marketing firm decides why and how it will collect website visitors' email addresses for a newsletter, and hires a cloud email provider that stores and sends the messages only on the firm's documented instructions. Under GDPR, which roles apply to the firm and the provider?",c:["Firm = processor; provider = controller","Firm = joint controller; provider = joint controller","Firm = controller; provider = processor","Firm = controller; provider = sub-processor"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. This reverses the roles. The firm determines the purpose and means, so it is the controller. The provider would be a controller only if it decided on its own why and how to use the email addresses.","Incorrect. Joint controllers jointly determine purposes and means. Here only the firm decides and the provider acts on documented instructions. Joint controllership would fit if both organizations set the newsletter's purposes together.","Correct. The firm determines the purpose and means of the processing, making it the controller, while the provider handles the data solely on the firm's documented instructions, making it a processor.","Incorrect. A sub-processor is engaged by a processor, not directly by the controller. The provider was hired by the firm itself, so it is a processor. It would be a sub-processor if another processor had hired it."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"Within one company, a VP of Sales is accountable for the CRM data, decides it is Confidential and approves who may access it, while IT administers the database and its backups. Which internal role pairing is correct?",c:["VP = data owner; IT = data custodian","VP = data steward; IT = data owner","VP = data controller; IT = data processor","VP = data custodian; IT = data steward"],a:0,e:["Correct. The owner is accountable for the data and decides its classification and access, while the custodian performs the technical administration and backups on the owner's behalf.","Incorrect. A steward manages quality and definitions rather than holding final accountability and access approval, and IT does not own data it merely hosts. The VP would be a steward if the role focused on data quality.","Incorrect. Controller and processor are GDPR legal roles for organizations in a processing relationship, not the internal accountability split described. They would fit if a separate vendor processed the CRM data for the company.","Incorrect. This swaps the technical and business roles: IT performs the custodian duties and the VP holds accountability. The VP would be a custodian only if they personally administered storage and backups."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"Which statement BEST distinguishes a data steward from a data custodian in a governance program?",c:["Stewards decide why data is collected and how it is processed; custodians process it on their behalf","Stewards are accountable for the data and set its classification; custodians apply that classification","Stewards run storage, backup and access controls; custodians govern data quality, definitions and permitted use across the business","Stewards govern data quality, definitions and permitted use; custodians run storage, backup and access controls"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Deciding why and how personal data is processed describes a GDPR controller, with a processor acting on its behalf. Stewards and custodians are internal governance roles, so this fits controller versus processor instead.","Incorrect. Accountability and classification decisions belong to the data owner, and custodians do apply them, but stewards are not the accountable party. This describes owner versus custodian.","Incorrect. This swaps the two roles. Technical storage, backup and access control are custodian duties, while quality, definitions and permitted use are the steward's business-facing responsibilities.","Correct. Stewards are business-facing, ensuring data quality, shared definitions and proper use, while custodians handle the technical side: storage, backup and access protection."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A federal agency is buying a hardware security module. It must confirm that the cryptographic module itself was tested by an accredited laboratory against a government cryptographic standard. Which requirement applies?",c:["Common Criteria EAL4+ evaluation by an accredited lab","FIPS 140-3 validation under the CMVP","NIST SP 800-53 control baseline","FedRAMP Moderate authorization"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Common Criteria evaluates a product's security functions against a protection profile and is not the cryptographic module validation program. It fits when procurement requires evaluated security products generally.","Correct. FIPS 140-3, validated through the Cryptographic Module Validation Program by accredited labs, is the government standard for cryptographic modules used by federal agencies.","Incorrect. SP 800-53 is a catalog of security and privacy controls for information systems, not a test of a specific module. It fits when an agency selects a control baseline for a system.","Incorrect. FedRAMP authorizes cloud service offerings for federal use and does not validate a hardware module's cryptography. It would apply if the agency were buying a cloud service instead of an HSM."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A publicly traded company's CFO must certify the effectiveness of internal controls over financial reporting, and external auditors test those controls every year. Which requirement drives this?",c:["Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act (GLBA) Safeguards Rule","Federal Information Security Modernization Act (FISMA)","Sarbanes-Oxley Act (SOX) Sections 302 and 404","Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS)"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. GLBA requires financial institutions to safeguard and disclose their handling of customer financial information. It does not mandate CFO certification of reporting controls. It fits a bank's privacy and safeguards duty.","Incorrect. FISMA requires federal agencies and their contractors to secure information systems. It does not govern public-company financial reporting. It fits a federal system needing authorization.","Correct. SOX sections 302 and 404 require executive certification and auditor attestation of internal controls over financial reporting for public companies.","Incorrect. PCI DSS is a contractual standard for protecting cardholder data and is assessed by QSAs, not financial auditors. It fits an organization that stores, processes or transmits payment cards."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A bank must give customers a notice explaining how it collects and shares their nonpublic personal financial information with affiliates and third parties, and must offer an opt-out for some sharing. Which law requires this?",c:["Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act Privacy Rule","Sarbanes-Oxley Act Section 404","HIPAA Privacy Rule for covered entities","Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act"],a:0,e:["Correct. The GLBA Privacy Rule requires financial institutions to deliver privacy notices and to offer opt-out rights for certain sharing of nonpublic personal information.","Incorrect. SOX Section 404 concerns management's assessment of internal controls over financial reporting, not customer privacy notices. It would apply to a public company's financial statement controls.","Incorrect. The HIPAA Privacy Rule governs protected health information held by covered entities. It would apply if the bank were acting as a health plan handling patient records.","Incorrect. FERPA protects student education records at institutions receiving federal funds. It would apply if the data were student records instead of customer financial information."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"An organization wants a voluntary, outcome-based framework with no certification, organized around six functions: Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond and Recover. Which framework fits?",c:["ISO/IEC 27001:2022 ISMS standard","CIS Critical Security Controls v8","NIST Risk Management Framework (SP 800-37)","NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) version 2.0"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. ISO/IEC 27001 specifies ISMS requirements and is certifiable by accredited auditors, which this organization does not want. It fits when customers demand a certificate.","Incorrect. CIS Controls v8 is voluntary, but it is organized as 18 prioritized controls rather than the six functions. It fits when the goal is a prescriptive list of hardening priorities.","Incorrect. The RMF is a seven-step lifecycle process required for US federal systems, not a function-based framework. It fits authorizing a federal information system.","Correct. CSF 2.0 is voluntary and outcome-based, has no formal certification, and adds Govern to the original five functions."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A cloud provider wants customers to see that its security management system meets an internationally recognized standard, and it wants a certificate issued by an accredited certification body. Which option is MOST appropriate?",c:["SOC 2 Type II attestation covering the Trust Services Criteria","ISO/IEC 27001 certification, with 27017 and 27018 guidance","NIST CSF 2.0 profile with a self-assessed implementation tier","FedRAMP authorization at the Moderate impact baseline"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. SOC 2 Type II is an attestation report from a CPA firm about control operation, not a certificate against an international ISMS standard. It fits when US customers ask for an auditor's report.","Correct. ISO/IEC 27001 is an international ISMS standard certified by accredited bodies. ISO 27017 adds cloud-specific controls and 27018 adds PII protection for public clouds.","Incorrect. NIST CSF has no certification, and an implementation tier is self-assessed. It fits when the goal is internal improvement and communication of cyber risk posture.","Incorrect. FedRAMP is a US government authorization program for cloud services, not an international certificate. It fits when the provider is selling to federal agencies."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A company with centralized security governance finds regional offices ignoring its policies. The regions say they were never consulted and that no one is accountable for compliance, yet a regulator requires consistent controls across every office. What is the MOST effective governance improvement?",c:["Switch to decentralized governance in which each region writes its own policies and self-assesses compliance","Rewrite the policies as short guidelines, post them on the intranet and send monthly reminders to regional managers","Form a governance committee with regional representatives, name policy owners and audit compliance each quarter","Move enforcement to an external managed provider that monitors regional endpoints against the policies under an SLA"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Fully decentralized, self-assessed policy would produce inconsistent controls and conflict with the regulator's consistency requirement. Decentralization fits when regions face different laws and consistency is not mandated.","Incorrect. Guidelines are optional, and reminders create neither involvement nor accountability. This addresses awareness, not the missing consultation and ownership. It would fit if staff simply did not know the policies existed.","Correct. Regional representation fixes the consultation gap, named owners create accountability, and periodic audits verify consistent compliance across all offices.","Incorrect. Endpoint monitoring may detect drift, but it gives regions no voice and assigns no accountability, and governance itself cannot be outsourced. It fits as technical enforcement once governance roles exist."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"Which statement BEST describes the relationship between a security policy and a security standard?",c:["A policy states management's intent; a standard sets mandatory, measurable requirements that carry it out","A standard states management's intent; a policy sets mandatory, measurable requirements that carry it out","A policy sets recommended practices; a standard sets mandatory step-by-step actions that carry them out","A policy lists mandatory technical settings; a standard gives optional advice for teams that want to exceed them"],a:0,e:["Correct. Policies are high-level statements of management intent, and standards operationalize them with mandatory, specific, measurable requirements such as required encryption algorithms.","Incorrect. This reverses the documents: the policy is the high-level statement and the standard is the more specific one. It would be right only if standards were the top-tier governance document.","Incorrect. Policies are mandatory, not recommended, and mandatory step-by-step actions are procedures, not standards. Recommended practices describe guidelines.","Incorrect. Technical settings belong in standards, and optional advice is a guideline. Policies stay high-level and mandatory. This description fits a standards-versus-guidelines split."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A company writes an incident response POLICY and a separate PLAYBOOK for ransomware. What is the difference between the two documents?",c:["The policy defines detection rules and tooling; the playbook defines legal reporting obligations after a breach","The policy covers preparation before incidents; the playbook covers lessons learned after incidents close","The policy sequences the ransomware response actions; the playbook assigns authority and mandates a response capability","The policy assigns authority and mandates a response capability; the playbook sequences actions for a ransomware event"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Detection rules and tooling live in technical standards and procedures, and legal reporting is part of the response process. Neither document is defined this way. It would fit a SIEM standard versus a notification procedure.","Incorrect. Preparation and lessons learned are phases of the incident response lifecycle, not the distinction between a policy and a playbook. Both documents concern the handling of incidents.","Incorrect. This reverses them. Authority and the mandate for a response capability are governance content in the policy, while scenario-specific sequenced actions belong in the playbook.","Correct. The policy is governance: it grants authority and requires a response capability. The playbook is procedure: it lays out specific step-by-step actions for a given scenario such as ransomware."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A US-based company stores and processes personal data of EU residents on servers in the United States. Which external consideration MOST directly shapes its security and privacy program?",c:["Industry: peer benchmarks such as sector security-maturity surveys and trade association guidance","Legal: data protection law with extraterritorial reach and rules on cross-border personal data transfers","Local: municipal ordinances governing data center siting, zoning and utility permits","Geopolitical: export controls and sanctions that restrict which countries may receive encryption technology"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Industry benchmarks are voluntary and do not bind the company to obligations about EU residents' data. They matter when comparing security maturity with peers.","Correct. GDPR applies extraterritorially to processing the data of people in the EU and restricts transfers to third countries, so legal obligations drive the program.","Incorrect. Municipal ordinances affect facilities, not how EU residents' personal data must be handled. They matter when building or leasing a data center in a particular city.","Incorrect. Export controls restrict transfers of technology, not the processing of EU residents' personal data. They matter when shipping or licensing encryption products abroad."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"Auditors will later ask who made each decision during incident handling and when, to confirm the response followed regulatory requirements. Which activity provides the BEST evidence?",c:["Capturing full packets from the affected network segment and retaining them for 30 days","Running a post-incident vulnerability scan of every host involved in the event","Keeping time-stamped, tamper-evident records of actions, decisions and approvals","Producing a lessons-learned report that lists root causes and recommended control improvements"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Packet captures show network activity, not who decided what or why. They fit forensic reconstruction of attacker traffic rather than demonstrating regulatory process compliance.","Incorrect. A scan shows the technical state of hosts after the event, not the decision trail. It fits when validating that remediation closed vulnerabilities.","Correct. An audit trail of time-stamped, tamper-evident actions and approvals documents who decided what and when, which is exactly what auditors need to test compliance.","Incorrect. A lessons-learned report is written afterward for improvement and does not capture decisions as they happened. It fits the post-incident review phase."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"Which key management practice MOST reduces the amount of data exposed if a single encryption key is compromised at some point in the future?",c:["Rotate keys on a defined cryptoperiod so each key protects limited data","Store keys in a hardware security module so they cannot be exported in plaintext","Escrow copies of keys with a trusted third party so data stays recoverable","Increase key length to 4096 bits so brute-force recovery becomes infeasible"],a:0,e:["Correct. Rotation on a defined cryptoperiod limits how much data and how much time any one key covers, so a compromise exposes only a limited window of data.","Incorrect. An HSM lowers the chance that a key is stolen but does not limit how much data a stolen key protects. It fits when theft of keys from servers is the main concern.","Incorrect. Escrow improves recoverability and increases the number of key copies, which does not limit exposure. It fits when losing a key could make data permanently unavailable.","Incorrect. Longer keys resist brute force, but a compromised key still decrypts everything it protected. It fits when cryptanalytic attack is the main threat."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"Which document describes how the organization will keep critical business functions running, including people, facilities, suppliers and manual workarounds and not just IT systems, during a prolonged disruption?",c:["Incident response plan (IRP)","Disaster recovery plan (DRP)","Business impact analysis (BIA) report","Business continuity plan (BCP)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. The incident response plan covers detecting, containing and recovering from security incidents, not sustaining every business function. It fits the handling of a cyberattack.","Incorrect. A disaster recovery plan restores IT systems and data after a disaster, so it is IT-focused. It fits when the question concerns recovering infrastructure.","Incorrect. A BIA identifies critical functions and quantifies impact and recovery objectives, but it does not describe how operations continue. It fits as the input that shapes the continuity plan.","Correct. The business continuity plan covers the whole business, maintaining critical functions through people, facilities, suppliers and workarounds during an extended disruption."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"A software company adopts a policy requiring threat modeling, secure code review and security testing before every release. Which policy is this?",c:["Change and release management policy","Secure development lifecycle policy","Vulnerability and patch management policy","Data classification and handling policy"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Change management governs how changes to production are requested, approved and rolled back. It does not mandate threat modeling or code review. It fits approval workflows for deployments.","Correct. A secure SDLC policy embeds security activities such as threat modeling, code review and testing into each stage of development before release.","Incorrect. Vulnerability management covers finding, prioritizing and remediating weaknesses in deployed assets, not design-time activities. It fits scanning and patching cadences.","Incorrect. A data classification policy defines labels and handling rules for data. It fits specifying how Confidential data must be stored or shared."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"Under GDPR, a company engaged in large-scale systematic monitoring of individuals must designate an independent person who advises on compliance, monitors it and acts as the contact point for the supervisory authority. Which role is this?",c:["Data owner (business accountable)","Data steward (quality and definitions)","Data Protection Officer (DPO) role","Chief Information Security Officer (CISO)"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A data owner is accountable for a dataset's classification and access, but is not an independent compliance advisor and regulator contact. It fits internal accountability for specific data.","Incorrect. A steward manages data quality and definitions, with no independence requirement or regulator-facing duty. It fits business-side data quality responsibility.","Correct. GDPR Article 37 requires a DPO for large-scale systematic monitoring. The DPO operates independently, advises on compliance and serves as the supervisory authority's contact.","Incorrect. A CISO leads the security program inside management, which conflicts with the DPO's required independence. It fits the executive who runs security operations and strategy."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"Which monitoring approach BEST verifies continuing compliance with a configuration standard across thousands of endpoints with limited staff?",c:["Automated compliance scanning against a baseline with centralized reporting","Quarterly manual sampling of five percent of endpoints by the internal audit team","Annual third-party audit of the endpoint management process and its documentation","Attestations from endpoint owners collected during security awareness training"],a:0,e:["Correct. Automated scanning against a baseline checks every endpoint continuously and reports centrally, which scales to thousands of devices with little staff.","Incorrect. Sampling covers a small fraction of devices and only periodically, so drift between samples goes unseen. It fits small fleets or controls that cannot be checked automatically.","Incorrect. An annual audit of process and documentation is point-in-time and does not examine each endpoint's actual state. It fits independent assurance over the management program.","Incorrect. Attestations are self-reported and unverified. They fit policy acknowledgment, not technical verification of settings."]},
{o:"5.1",q:"Which option BEST describes the difference between due diligence and due care in governance?",c:["Due diligence applies only to vendor selection; due care applies only to employee conduct","Due care is investigating risks before acting; due diligence is taking reasonable action to manage them","Due diligence is a legally mandated obligation; due care is a voluntary best practice for mature programs","Due diligence is investigating and understanding risks; due care is acting reasonably to address them"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Both concepts apply broadly across the organization, including investments, systems and vendors. Restricting each to one area misstates them. Vendor investigation is only one example of due diligence.","Incorrect. This reverses the terms. Investigating and understanding risk is due diligence, and acting on it is due care. Reasonable investigation generally comes first.","Incorrect. Failing due care can create legal liability for negligence, so it is not merely voluntary. Neither term is defined by being mandated or voluntary.","Correct. Due diligence is the research and understanding of risks; due care is the reasonable action taken to address them, consistent with what a prudent organization would do."]},

// ---------- 5.2 (24) ----------
{o:"5.2",q:"A $200,000 file server faces a ransomware risk that would destroy 40% of its value per incident, and the incident is expected twice every five years. What is the annualized loss expectancy (ALE)?",c:["$80,000 (SLE of $80,000 × ARO of 1.0)","$16,000 (SLE of $80,000 × ARO of 0.2)","$32,000 (SLE of $80,000 × ARO of 0.4)","$160,000 (SLE of $80,000 × ARO of 2.0)"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. This treats the $80,000 SLE as an annual figure, which assumes one incident per year. Two incidents in five years is an ARO of 0.4, not 1.0. An ARO of 1.0 would give this answer.","Incorrect. An ARO of 0.2 corresponds to one incident every five years. The stem says two incidents in five years, so the frequency is double that.","Correct. SLE = $200,000 × 0.40 = $80,000. ARO = 2 incidents / 5 years = 0.4. ALE = SLE × ARO = $32,000.","Incorrect. An ARO of 2.0 means two incidents every year. The stem gives two incidents over five years, so the ARO is 0.4 and the ALE is far lower."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A control costs $25,000 per year and reduces a risk's ALE from $60,000 to $20,000. Is it cost-justified, and what is its annual value?",c:["Yes; net benefit of $35,000 per year (original ALE of $60,000 minus the $25,000 cost)","Yes; net benefit of $15,000 per year ($40,000 ALE reduction minus the $25,000 cost)","No; the $25,000 cost exceeds the remaining ALE of $20,000, so it loses money","No; net loss of $5,000 per year (the $25,000 cost minus the $20,000 residual ALE)"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. This subtracts the cost from the original ALE, ignoring that $20,000 of expected loss remains. Only the reduction in ALE counts as the control's benefit.","Correct. Control value = (ALE before - ALE after) - annual cost = ($60,000 - $20,000) - $25,000 = $15,000 net benefit, so it is cost-justified.","Incorrect. The control's cost should be compared with the reduction in ALE ($40,000), not with the remaining ALE. Comparing against the residual figure wrongly rejects a positive-value control.","Incorrect. This compares cost with the residual ALE, which is not the benefit. The benefit is the $40,000 reduction, so the net result is a $15,000 gain, not a loss."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"An asset worth $500,000 has an exposure factor of 10% and an ARO of 0.25. A proposed control costs $15,000 annually and would reduce the ARO to 0.05. Is it cost-effective?",c:["Yes; ALE falls by $100,000 per year, which is more than the $15,000 annual cost","No; ALE falls by $2,500 per year, which is less than the $15,000 annual cost","Yes; ALE falls by $40,000 per year, which is more than the $15,000 annual cost","No; ALE falls by $10,000 per year, which is less than the $15,000 annual cost"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. This multiplies the ARO change (0.20) by the full $500,000 asset value, ignoring the 10% exposure factor. The SLE is $50,000, so the reduction is much smaller.","Incorrect. $2,500 is the residual ALE after the control (0.05 × $50,000), not the reduction. The actual saving is the difference between the before and after ALE.","Incorrect. This assumes the control cuts the SLE from $50,000 to $10,000, but the control changes only the frequency (ARO), not the loss per incident.","Correct. SLE = $500,000 × 10% = $50,000. ALE before = 0.25 × $50,000 = $12,500; after = 0.05 × $50,000 = $2,500. The $10,000 reduction is below the $15,000 cost."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A team scores each risk with likelihood (Rare, Possible, Likely) and impact (Low, Medium, High) on a heat map, with no dollar figures. Which analysis method is this?",c:["Qualitative risk analysis using ordinal likelihood and impact category ratings","Quantitative risk analysis using SLE, ARO and ALE values","Monte Carlo simulation using repeated random sampling of loss distributions","Delphi technique using anonymous rounds of expert estimates"],a:0,e:["Correct. Rating likelihood and impact with ordinal categories on a heat map, without monetary values, is qualitative analysis.","Incorrect. Quantitative analysis assigns numeric and monetary values such as SLE, ARO and ALE. It would fit if the team had dollar-based loss estimates instead of category labels.","Incorrect. Monte Carlo is a quantitative technique that samples probability distributions to model outcomes. It would fit if the team modeled loss ranges numerically rather than using categories.","Incorrect. Delphi is a way of gathering consensus estimates from experts through anonymous rounds. It could feed qualitative scoring, but it is not the scoring method described."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A board states: 'We will pursue aggressive growth in new markets and accept moderate cybersecurity risk to do so.' This statement defines the organization's:",c:["Risk tolerance (acceptable deviation around an objective)","Risk threshold (specific trigger point that prompts action)","Risk appetite (broad level of risk sought to meet strategic goals)","Residual risk (exposure remaining after controls are applied)"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Tolerance is the acceptable variation around a specific objective, set below the strategic level. It would fit a statement such as 'downtime may vary by up to four hours per quarter'.","Incorrect. A threshold is a specific measurable point that triggers escalation or action. The board statement sets no trigger value.","Correct. Risk appetite is the broad, strategic amount of risk leadership is willing to pursue to achieve its objectives, as in this board statement.","Incorrect. Residual risk is what remains after controls are applied to a specific risk. The statement describes a strategic stance, not the leftover exposure of a particular risk."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"Operations allows up to 4 hours of downtime per quarter for the e-commerce site; beyond that, executives must be notified and a remediation plan created. Which terms apply to '4 hours' and to 'beyond that'?",c:["Risk appetite for the 4 hours; residual risk beyond it, triggering a formal review","Risk tolerance for the 4 hours; risk threshold beyond it, triggering executive escalation","RTO for the 4 hours; MTD beyond it, triggering a failover to the DR site","Inherent risk for the 4 hours; KRI beyond it, triggering a management review"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Appetite is a strategic, organization-wide stance, not a quarterly allowance for one site, and residual risk is leftover exposure rather than a trigger. Appetite would fit a board's growth-versus-risk statement.","Correct. The 4 hours is the acceptable variation (tolerance) for the objective, and crossing it is the threshold that triggers executive notification and a remediation plan.","Incorrect. RTO and MTD apply to a single outage and its recovery, not to cumulative downtime per quarter. They would fit a BIA of how fast one outage must be restored.","Incorrect. Inherent risk is exposure before controls, and a KRI is a metric rather than a limit. The stem sets an allowed amount and an escalation point, not a baseline or a metric."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"Which of the following is a KEY RISK INDICATOR (KRI) rather than a key performance indicator (KPI)?",c:["Percentage of vulnerability scans completed on schedule, holding at 98%","Number of help desk tickets closed per analyst per week, trending upward","Percentage of employees finishing awareness training before the deadline, trending upward","Percentage of critical servers on unsupported operating systems, trending upward"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Scan completion on schedule measures how well a process is performing, which makes it a KPI. It would be a KRI only if it signaled growing exposure.","Incorrect. Tickets closed per analyst measures team productivity, a KPI. It says little about rising exposure to loss.","Incorrect. Training completion measures program execution, a KPI. It becomes risk-related only when tied to behavior outcomes.","Correct. A rising share of critical servers on unsupported operating systems is a forward-looking signal of growing exposure, which is what a KRI measures."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A risk register entry lists the risk, owner, likelihood, impact and the response 'mitigate via MFA rollout by Q3'. Auditors want to see whether the response is actually reducing exposure. Which field is MISSING?",c:["Residual risk rating and current status of the response","Cost-benefit analysis for each alternative response considered","Date and author of the board's risk appetite statement","Recovery time objective for each affected business process"],a:0,e:["Correct. Registers track the residual rating and the response status so progress and remaining exposure are visible to auditors and management.","Incorrect. A cost-benefit analysis supports choosing a response but is not a per-entry field showing whether the chosen response is working. It fits a response selection record.","Incorrect. The appetite statement is organization-level context, not an entry field. It is used to judge residual risk, not to track progress.","Incorrect. RTO comes from the BIA and is relevant to continuity planning. It does not show whether this risk's response is reducing exposure."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"After rolling out MFA, the likelihood of account takeover drops but not to zero. What is the remaining risk called, and what should management do with it?",c:["Inherent risk; document it as the baseline and close the register entry","Transferred risk; buy cyber insurance to cover whatever remains","Residual risk; accept it formally if within appetite, or add further controls","Secondary risk; assign it to the vendor of the MFA product"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Inherent risk is exposure before any controls, and closing the entry would hide the remaining exposure. Inherent risk is the baseline against which control effectiveness is measured.","Incorrect. Insurance is one possible response but not automatic or mandatory, and it is not what the remaining risk is called. It fits when residual loss exceeds appetite and cannot be reduced further.","Correct. Residual risk is the exposure left after controls. Management compares it to appetite and either formally accepts it or applies additional controls.","Incorrect. Secondary risk is a new risk created by a control, such as users bypassing MFA. The remaining account takeover risk is the original risk, reduced."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"Which business impact analysis (BIA) output directly determines how frequently a system must be backed up or replicated?",c:["Maximum tolerable downtime (MTD)","Recovery point objective (RPO)","Recovery time objective (RTO)","Mean time between failures (MTBF)"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. MTD is the longest outage the business can survive and sets the ceiling for recovery time. It would drive recovery architecture, not backup frequency.","Correct. The RPO is the maximum acceptable data loss measured in time, so it sets how often data must be backed up or replicated.","Incorrect. The RTO sets how quickly service must be restored, which drives failover and recovery methods rather than how often data is copied.","Incorrect. MTBF measures reliability between failures. It informs availability planning but is not a BIA objective that sets backup frequency."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A payroll system's BIA shows that after 48 hours of total downtime, penalties and harm to employees become unacceptable. How should the 48 hours be treated?",c:["Recovery point objective, with backups taken at least every 48 hours","Work recovery time, with the RTO set equal to 48 hours","Mean time to repair, with spare parts stocked for 48 hours","Maximum tolerable downtime, with the RTO set below 48 hours"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. An RPO describes acceptable data loss, not outage duration. Backups every 48 hours would also allow up to two days of lost payroll data.","Incorrect. Work recovery time is the period after systems are restored to verify data and resume operations. MTD equals RTO plus WRT, so setting RTO to the full 48 hours leaves no time for it.","Incorrect. MTTR describes the average repair time of a component, not the business's absolute limit. Stocking spares does not set a recovery objective.","Correct. The point at which the outage becomes unacceptable is the maximum tolerable downtime. The RTO, plus work recovery time, must fit inside it, so the RTO must be shorter than 48 hours."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A system has an MTBF of 10,000 hours and an MTTR of 10 hours. If the MTTR grows to 100 hours with the MTBF unchanged, what happens to availability?",c:["It falls from about 99.9% to about 99.0% because repair time becomes a larger share of each failure cycle","It rises from about 99.0% to about 99.9% because longer repairs are more thorough and prevent failures","It stays near 99.9% because availability depends only on the MTBF of the system","It falls from about 99.9% to about 90.9% because 100 hours of repair is compared with 1,000 hours of uptime"],a:0,e:["Correct. Availability = MTBF / (MTBF + MTTR). It is 10,000 / 10,010 ≈ 99.9% before and 10,000 / 10,100 ≈ 99.0% after, so longer repairs reduce uptime.","Incorrect. Longer repair time does not prevent failures, and the direction is reversed. MTBF, which reflects failure frequency, is unchanged in the stem.","Incorrect. Availability depends on both MTBF and MTTR. If MTTR did not matter, repair speed would never affect uptime.","Incorrect. This uses 1,000 hours as uptime instead of the stated 10,000 hours. With the correct MTBF the drop is from about 99.9% to about 99.0%."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A company performs a risk assessment only after a breach occurs. Which assessment type is this, and what is the main problem with relying on it?",c:["Continuous assessment; findings change so frequently that they lose relevance","Recurring assessment; fixed intervals make results predictable and stale between cycles","Ad hoc, reactive assessment; risks are identified too late to prevent incidents","One-time assessment; a single deep review cannot stay current after the first use"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Continuous assessment monitors risk on an ongoing basis. The company here assesses only after an event, which is the opposite pattern.","Incorrect. Recurring assessments run on a schedule, such as annually. This company has no schedule and acts only after a breach.","Correct. Assessments triggered by an event are ad hoc and reactive, so risks are found after harm has occurred instead of being treated beforehand.","Incorrect. A one-time assessment is a planned, single evaluation for a specific purpose such as a go-live. Post-breach assessment is event-driven and can recur with each incident."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A firm stops offering a mobile app because its risk exceeds the company's appetite and the available mitigations are too costly. Which risk response is this?",c:["Risk transference (shift financial impact to a third party)","Risk avoidance (eliminate the risky activity entirely)","Risk mitigation (reduce likelihood or impact with controls)","Risk acceptance (retain the risk with management approval)"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Transference moves financial impact through insurance or contracts while the activity continues. Here the company stops the activity.","Correct. Discontinuing the activity removes the exposure altogether, which is risk avoidance, chosen when risk exceeds appetite and mitigation is not worth the cost.","Incorrect. Mitigation reduces likelihood or impact with controls while the app continues. The stem says mitigations were too costly and the app was withdrawn.","Incorrect. Acceptance keeps the activity and its risk with management sign-off. The firm declined to keep the risk by ending the app."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"Which statement about risk transference is MOST accurate?",c:["Legal liability for a breach moves to the insurer once the policy is purchased, including regulatory fines","Transference applies only to physical risks such as fire, so cyber risk must be mitigated or avoided","Transference removes the need for security controls, since the insurer takes over monitoring duties","Insurance or contracts can shift financial impact, but accountability and reputational harm remain"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Insurance may cover certain costs, but legal liability and some regulatory penalties are not always insurable, and the organization stays accountable.","Incorrect. Cyber insurance exists, and cyber risk can be transferred financially. Transference is not limited to physical risks.","Incorrect. Insurers typically require controls as a condition of coverage, and a policy does not shift monitoring responsibility.","Correct. Insurance and contracts can transfer the financial impact, but the organization keeps accountability to customers and regulators and suffers reputational damage."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"An organization identifies risks, analyzes them and selects responses. Which risk management step ensures the responses stay effective as conditions change?",c:["Initial risk identification using workshops and interviews","Quantitative analysis of likelihood and impact values","Ongoing monitoring and review of risks and controls","Selection of a response by the designated risk owner"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Identification finds risks at a point in time. It would not detect changes in the environment after the responses were chosen unless repeated.","Incorrect. Analysis estimates likelihood and impact when performed, but it does not by itself track whether responses remain effective.","Correct. Monitoring and review track risks, control performance and indicators over time and trigger reassessment as threats, systems and business conditions change.","Incorrect. Choosing a response is a decision at one moment. It does not verify afterward whether the response still works."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"Which approach to a policy exemption is BEST?",c:["Written approval from the requesting manager, recorded in the ticket and valid until the system is retired","Time-boxed verbal approval from the security team, confirmed by email within 30 days","Documented justification, compensating controls, a named owner and an expiration date with review","Approval by the CISO with documented justification and no further review until the next annual audit"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. The requester approving their own exception lacks independence, and a no-expiry exemption is never revisited. It would fit only a retiring system with separate risk sign-off.","Incorrect. Verbal approval is not an auditable record, and confirmation up to 30 days later leaves a gap. Written approval at the time of decision is expected.","Correct. A governed exemption has documented business justification, compensating controls, accountable ownership and an expiration date so it is reviewed rather than becoming permanent.","Incorrect. CISO approval with justification is a good start, but without compensating controls, ownership and periodic review the exception can linger unnoticed until the annual audit."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"Which scenario BEST describes inherent risk?",c:["The chance of a breach on a web application after a WAF and MFA are deployed","The chance of a breach on a new web application before any controls are applied","The chance of a breach on a web application once cyber insurance covers the losses","The chance of a breach on a web application that the board has formally accepted"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Risk remaining after controls such as a WAF and MFA is residual risk. Inherent risk is measured before controls.","Correct. Inherent risk is the level of risk that exists before any controls or other treatments are applied.","Incorrect. Insurance transfers the financial impact but does not change the underlying chance of a breach. It describes the transference response, not inherent risk.","Incorrect. A risk the board has formally accepted is a residual risk that was accepted. Inherent risk is defined by the absence of controls, not by a decision."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A company rates a risk: likelihood 4 out of 5 and impact 5 out of 5, producing a score of 20 labeled 'Critical'. Which method is this?",c:["Quantitative analysis using dollar-based SLE and ARO values","Monte Carlo simulation using repeated random sampling of loss distributions","Delphi technique using anonymous rounds of expert estimates","Semi-quantitative analysis using numeric scores on qualitative ratings"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Quantitative analysis produces monetary figures such as SLE and ALE. A 1-to-5 score of 20 has no dollar meaning.","Incorrect. Monte Carlo simulates many outcomes from probability distributions. A single multiplied score does not use sampling.","Incorrect. Delphi gathers consensus estimates from experts. It may inform the ratings, but it does not describe multiplying scales into a score.","Correct. Assigning numbers to qualitative ratings and combining them (4 × 5 = 20) is semi-quantitative analysis, which is common in risk matrices."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A significant residual risk affecting the revenue of a business unit must be formally accepted. Who should accept it?",c:["The business executive who owns the affected process and its budget","The security architect who designed the control that reduced the risk","The internal auditor who reported the remaining exposure to management","The third-party vendor whose product contributes to the remaining exposure"],a:0,e:["Correct. Acceptance belongs to the risk owner with the authority and accountability to accept the exposure on behalf of the business unit.","Incorrect. The architect designed and understands the control but typically lacks authority over the business unit's budget and risk decisions. The architect advises the owner.","Incorrect. Internal audit assesses and reports independently. Accepting a risk would compromise audit independence.","Incorrect. A vendor is not accountable for the company's risk and cannot accept it on the company's behalf. Contract terms may share liability, but the acceptance decision stays internal."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"An organization's risk report shows a 'phishing click rate' rising from 3% to 9% in two quarters. What type of metric is this, and what action follows?",c:["KPI; reward departments whose training completion meets target without changing controls","ALE; purchase insurance sized to the 9% click rate","KRI; investigate causes and strengthen training and technical email controls","MTTR; reduce time to remediate compromised mailboxes"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A click rate measures exposure, not program performance, so it behaves as a KRI. Rewarding completion alone ignores the rising risk signal.","Incorrect. ALE is a monetary expected loss, and a click rate is a percentage. Insurance does not address the underlying trend.","Correct. A rising click rate signals growing exposure to phishing, which makes it a key risk indicator. Management should investigate causes and strengthen training and controls.","Incorrect. MTTR measures how fast repairs are made. It would apply to remediation speed, not to the rate at which users fall for phishing."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A system with an ARO of 0.1 and an SLE of $50,000 is compared with another system with an ARO of 2 and an SLE of $3,000. Which statement is correct?",c:["The first system has the higher ALE, at $50,000 versus $6,000, because the SLE is the annual loss figure","The second system has the higher ALE, at $6,000 versus $5,000, once each SLE is multiplied by its ARO","Both have the same ALE of $6,000, because a higher ARO offsets a lower SLE in this comparison","The first system has the higher ALE, at $5,000 versus $3,000, because ARO applies only once per system"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. The SLE is the loss per incident, not annual loss. ALE multiplies SLE by ARO, giving $5,000 for the first system and $6,000 for the second.","Correct. ALE = SLE × ARO. First system: 0.1 × $50,000 = $5,000. Second system: 2 × $3,000 = $6,000. The second is higher.","Incorrect. The first system's ALE is $5,000, not $6,000. A higher frequency does not make the two equal here.","Incorrect. ARO applies to each system's SLE, and the second system's ALE is $6,000, not $3,000. Ignoring its ARO of 2 yields this error."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"What is the BEST reason to assign a named risk owner rather than listing 'IT Department' in the risk register?",c:["A named owner is required by quantitative analysis, because ALE cannot be computed for a department","A named owner lets the register be sorted by department, which is needed to calculate each ALE","Assigning an individual transfers the legal liability for the risk from the company to that person","A single person is accountable for tracking the risk and for deciding and funding its response"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. ALE is calculated from asset value, exposure and frequency, not from who owns the risk. Quantitative analysis does not need individual names.","Incorrect. Sorting by department needs a department field, not a named person, and ALE does not depend on sorting.","Incorrect. Naming an owner does not shift the organization's legal liability to an individual. It assigns accountability within the organization.","Correct. A named individual has clear accountability and authority to monitor the risk and carry out its response, whereas a department leaves ownership diffuse."]},
{o:"5.2",q:"A risk assessment is scheduled as a project milestone immediately before a major ERP go-live to baseline the risk of that specific deployment, and it will not be repeated afterward. How is this assessment BEST described?",c:["One-time, single-event assessment","Continuous, automated assessment cycle","Recurring, scheduled assessment cycle","Ad hoc, event-driven assessment cycle"],a:0,e:["Correct. A single, planned assessment for one specific event, not repeated afterward, is a one-time assessment.","Incorrect. Continuous assessment monitors risk on an ongoing basis. This assessment is a single point-in-time effort.","Incorrect. Recurring assessments repeat on a schedule such as annually. This one is planned to occur once.","Incorrect. Ad hoc assessments are triggered by unplanned events, such as an incident or a new threat. This one was scheduled in the project plan."]},

// ---------- 5.3 (16) ----------
{o:"5.3",q:"A company and an MSP signed a master agreement covering liability, payment terms and dispute resolution. Six months later the company wants the MSP to migrate its email, with defined deliverables, milestones and a deadline. Which documents govern the migration?",c:["A new SLA that replaces the terms of the existing master agreement","A new MOU plus an NDA covering the migration project","A BPA recording each party's share of the migration costs","The existing MSA plus a new SOW for the migration deliverables and milestones"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. An SLA defines service levels such as uptime and response times, and it would not replace the MSA's liability and payment terms. It fits ongoing performance commitments, not a project with deliverables.","Incorrect. An MOU records mutual intent and is usually not a binding scope document, and an NDA covers confidentiality only. Together they do not define deliverables, milestones or a deadline.","Incorrect. A BPA sets out the terms of a business partnership, such as profit sharing. It does not scope a vendor project under an existing master agreement.","Correct. The MSA remains the umbrella for liability and payment, and the SOW documents the specific work: deliverables, milestones and the deadline for the email migration."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"A cloud SLA promises 99.9% monthly uptime, with service credits for any month below that figure. In a 30-day month the service was down for 50 minutes in total. Was the SLA met?",c:["No; 99.9% allows about 43 minutes of downtime in a 30-day month, so 50 minutes is a breach","Yes; 99.9% allows about 4.3 hours of downtime in a 30-day month, so 50 minutes is within the SLA","Yes; 99.9% allows about 72 minutes of downtime in a 30-day month, so 50 minutes is within the SLA","No; 99.9% allows about 4 minutes of downtime in a 30-day month, so 50 minutes is a breach"],a:0,e:["Correct. A 30-day month has 43,200 minutes, and 0.1% of that is about 43.2 minutes. Fifty minutes of downtime exceeds the allowance, so the SLA is breached and credits apply.","Incorrect. 4.3 hours would be roughly 99.4% uptime, a decimal slip. At 99.9% the allowed downtime is about 43 minutes.","Incorrect. 72 minutes would correspond to about 99.83% uptime. The 99.9% commitment allows only about 43 minutes.","Incorrect. About 4 minutes is the allowance for 99.99% uptime, a different tier. At 99.9% the allowance is about 43 minutes, though the conclusion of a breach is the same."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"Before engaging a SaaS vendor that will hold moderately sensitive data, a company reviews the vendor's SOC 2 Type II report and finds an exception in user access reviews. What is the BEST next step?",c:["Accept the report as a clean opinion, because an auditor-issued SOC 2 Type II already covers access reviews","Request a SOC 2 Type I report instead, because design-only testing contains no operating exceptions","Ask the vendor for a remediation plan, assess compensating controls, and document a risk decision","Require the vendor to obtain ISO/IEC 27001 certification before signing, regardless of how severe the exception is"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A Type II report can still contain exceptions, and an exception means the control did not operate as intended. Treating it as clean ignores the finding.","Incorrect. A Type I report only evaluates control design at a point in time, so it gives weaker assurance. Switching to it hides the problem rather than resolving it.","Correct. Exceptions require follow-up: understand the cause, evaluate compensating controls and remediation, and make a documented risk decision or adjust contract terms.","Incorrect. Demanding certification regardless of severity is rigid and does not address the specific access review exception. It could be reasonable if risk assessment showed the gap was severe."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"Which statement correctly distinguishes a SOC 2 Type I report from a SOC 2 Type II report?",c:["Type I is issued by the vendor's management; Type II is issued by an independent CPA firm","Type I reports the design of controls at a point in time; Type II tests their operating effectiveness over a period","Type II reports the design of controls at a point in time; Type I tests their operating effectiveness over a period","Type I covers only the security criterion; Type II adds availability, confidentiality, processing integrity and privacy"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Both report types are issued by an independent service auditor, normally a CPA firm. Management provides a description and assertion in both.","Correct. Type I describes and evaluates the design of controls at a single date, while Type II also tests whether they operated effectively over a review period, giving stronger assurance.","Incorrect. This reverses the two types. Type II is the report that tests operating effectiveness over a period of time.","Incorrect. Either type can cover any of the Trust Services Criteria the vendor chooses. The difference is point-in-time design versus effectiveness over a period."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"Which contract clause MOST directly allows a company to verify a vendor's security controls for itself?",c:["Indemnification clause covering third-party losses","Force majeure clause covering extraordinary events","Source code escrow clause covering vendor failure","Right-to-audit clause covering vendor records"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Indemnification allocates responsibility for losses and legal costs. It compensates after a problem but does not let the customer verify controls.","Incorrect. Force majeure excuses performance during extraordinary events outside a party's control. It has nothing to do with verifying controls.","Incorrect. Escrow protects access to source code if the vendor fails. It fits business continuity concerns, not control verification.","Correct. A right-to-audit clause gives the customer the contractual right to inspect or have an auditor examine the vendor's controls and records."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"During vendor selection, a company discovers that the CEO of a leading vendor is the brother of the CIO, who is responsible for choosing the vendor. What is the BEST action?",c:["Disclose the relationship, recuse the CIO, and have an independent panel validate the decision","Proceed with the CIO's selection, because the vendor scored highest on the technical evaluation criteria","Keep the CIO on the evaluation but require a second signature from the CFO on final contract approval","Remove the vendor from consideration and restart the evaluation with a new set of candidates"],a:0,e:["Correct. Conflicts of interest are managed through disclosure, recusal of the conflicted person and independent validation, which protects the process regardless of the vendor's merit.","Incorrect. A strong technical score does not remove the conflict or its appearance. The decision must still be insulated from the CIO's personal interest.","Incorrect. An added signature is a partial control, but the conflicted CIO still shapes the evaluation. Recusal is needed to keep the process independent.","Incorrect. Automatically disqualifying a qualified vendor is an overreaction that may harm the business. Managing the conflict is sufficient and preferred."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"A consultant hired to recommend a firewall platform receives referral bonuses from one of the manufacturers being evaluated. Which type of conflict of interest is this?",c:["Personal relationship (family or friendship ties with a bidder)","Competitive interest (the consultant's employer competes with a bidder)","Financial interest (an incentive paid by a bidder)","Insider information (access to confidential bid data)"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A personal relationship conflict arises from family or friendship ties. No such ties are described.","Incorrect. A competitive conflict arises when the evaluator's employer competes with a bidder. The stem describes payments from one manufacturer.","Correct. Referral bonuses from a bidder create a monetary incentive that can bias the recommendation, which is a financial conflict of interest.","Incorrect. Insider information conflicts involve access to confidential data about bids or competitors. The stem describes compensation, not data access."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"Two government agencies agree to share threat intelligence. They want a non-binding document that records each party's intentions and responsibilities. No money changes hands. Which document fits?",c:["Statement of work defining deliverables and payment milestones","Memorandum of understanding recording each party's duties","Service-level agreement with financial penalties for missed targets","Non-disclosure agreement restricting use of shared information"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A SOW defines work for a vendor, including deliverables and payment. It does not document mutual non-financial responsibilities between agencies.","Correct. An MOU (or similar MOA) is a generally non-binding record of mutual intent and responsibilities between parties, with no payment involved.","Incorrect. An SLA with penalties is a commercial service contract. The agencies are not buying or selling a service.","Incorrect. An NDA protects confidentiality only. It could be added to the arrangement, but it does not record each party's responsibilities for sharing."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"Two agencies connect their networks with a dedicated link. Which document specifically defines the technical security requirements and responsibilities for that connection?",c:["Memorandum of understanding (MOU) of intent","Business partners agreement (BPA) for the venture","Statement of work (SOW) for the link build","Interconnection security agreement (ISA)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. An MOU records general intent and responsibilities between parties but does not set the technical security requirements of a connection.","Incorrect. A BPA governs a business partnership, such as profit sharing and responsibilities. It does not specify the security controls of a network link.","Incorrect. A SOW defines the scope of a project or service. It would fit work to build the link, not security requirements for operating it.","Correct. An ISA documents the technical and security requirements for connecting two systems, such as encryption, ports and incident notification."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"A company learns that its payroll vendor uses a subcontractor in another country to process employee data. Which vendor risk activity would have identified this earlier?",c:["Fourth-party risk review of the vendor's subcontractors during due diligence","Annual penetration test of the vendor's customer-facing portal","Review of the vendor's SLA uptime and response-time metrics","Tabletop exercise simulating a breach with the vendor's incident team"],a:0,e:["Correct. A supply chain analysis that looks at the vendor's own suppliers, the fourth parties, during due diligence reveals subcontractors that handle the company's data.","Incorrect. A pen test of the portal measures its technical exposure and does not map who else handles the data. It fits validating an application's security.","Incorrect. SLA metrics show performance, not which subcontractors process data. They fit monitoring service quality.","Incorrect. A tabletop exercise tests response coordination after a breach and would not reveal subcontractors by itself. It fits incident readiness."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"Which set of contract terms is MOST important from a security perspective when engaging a vendor that will handle customers' PII?",c:["Uptime percentage, response-time targets and service credits for missed targets","Payment schedule, invoicing terms and late-payment interest charges","Breach notification timeline, data handling duties and liability for regulatory penalties","Termination notice period, renewal terms and annual price escalation limits"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. These are service-level terms that govern availability and support. They matter commercially but do not set data protection duties.","Incorrect. Payment and invoicing are commercial terms with no effect on how PII is protected.","Correct. For a vendor processing PII, enforceable breach notification, data handling obligations and liability allocation create the security duties and recourse the company needs.","Incorrect. Termination and pricing terms are commercial and exit-related. They do not define how PII must be protected or reported if compromised."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"A vendor refuses to allow your team to run a penetration test but offers a recent third-party penetration test report. What is a reasonable approach?",c:["Terminate the evaluation, since any refusal of customer-run testing disqualifies a multi-tenant vendor","Review the independent report's scope, findings and remediation evidence as alternative assurance","Run an unannounced scan of the vendor's public IP ranges to validate the report's findings","Accept the report summary page as sufficient and skip reviewing the detailed findings and scope"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Many vendors, especially multi-tenant providers, cannot permit customer testing, so refusal alone is not disqualifying. This overreaction would remove viable vendors.","Correct. An independent report can provide adequate assurance if its scope, recency, findings and remediation status match the company's risk requirements.","Incorrect. Scanning without authorization can violate law and the contract, regardless of intent. Authorization and rules of engagement are required.","Incorrect. A summary may omit scope limits and unresolved findings. The detailed report must be reviewed to judge the assurance it provides."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"Which option BEST describes effective ongoing vendor monitoring?",c:["One thorough assessment at onboarding, repeated only when the contract is renegotiated or renewed","Annual review of the vendor's marketing materials and published security whitepapers","Quarterly vendor-provided attestations stating that no security changes have occurred","Periodic reassessments, SLA metric tracking, and monitoring for vendor breaches or ownership changes"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Reassessing only at renegotiation leaves long gaps during which the vendor's security posture may change.","Incorrect. Marketing materials are not objective evidence of controls. They do not replace independent assessment.","Incorrect. Self-reported attestations that nothing has changed are unverified and offer limited assurance. They can supplement but not replace independent checks.","Correct. Vendor risk changes over time, so effective monitoring combines scheduled reassessments, performance tracking and watching for incidents and changes in the vendor."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"A company asks vendors to complete a security questionnaire as part of its vendor risk program. What is the MAIN limitation of relying on it alone?",c:["Answers are self-reported and unverified unless backed by evidence like audit reports","Results cannot be compared across vendors because the questions are never standardized","The vendor must already hold a SOC 2 report before a questionnaire may be issued to it","Responses are valid only for the fiscal year in which the vendor's own financial audit closes"],a:0,e:["Correct. A questionnaire records what the vendor says, not what is true. Evidence such as audit reports or test results is needed to verify the answers.","Incorrect. Standardized questionnaires such as SIG or CAIQ exist precisely so results can be compared across vendors. The limitation is not comparability.","Incorrect. A questionnaire can be issued to any vendor. Requiring a SOC 2 report first is not a rule, though a SOC 2 report is useful evidence.","Incorrect. Validity is not tied to the vendor's audit year. The limitation is that responses are not verified, not that they expire on a fixed date."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"Which set of items MUST be included in the rules of engagement for a penetration test performed by an external vendor?",c:["Scope, tester qualifications, hourly rates, liability caps and invoice terms","Scope, testing windows, permitted techniques, points of contact and written authorization","Scope, network diagrams, source code access, credentials and architecture documents","Scope, vulnerability severity ratings, remediation owners, retest dates and a risk register"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. These are commercial and contract details that belong in a statement of work or master agreement, not the rules of engagement.","Correct. Rules of engagement define what may be tested, when, how, who to contact and the written authorization that makes the test legal.","Incorrect. Network diagrams, source code and credentials are inputs provided in a known-environment test. They are not required in every rules-of-engagement document.","Incorrect. Severity ratings, owners and retest dates belong to reporting and remediation after the test, not to the engagement rules."]},
{o:"5.3",q:"A business partner agreement for a joint venture between two companies should MOST likely address which set of terms?",c:["Interconnection ports, encryption requirements, incident notification and key management for the shared link","Uptime targets, response times, credit calculations and reporting cadence for the shared service","Profit and loss sharing, each party's responsibilities, IP ownership and dissolution terms","Testing scope, attack windows, permitted techniques and escalation contacts for security assessments"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. These are the contents of an interconnection security agreement, which covers the technical security of a connection between systems.","Incorrect. These belong in an SLA, which sets measurable service performance commitments.","Correct. A BPA establishes the business relationship of partners: how profits and losses are shared, who is responsible for what, who owns intellectual property and how the venture ends.","Incorrect. These belong in rules of engagement for a penetration test, not in the terms of a business partnership."]},

// ---------- 5.4 (14) ----------
{o:"5.4",q:"A healthcare provider loses a laptop holding 10,000 patient records encrypted with AES-256, but the decryption key was stored in a text file on the same laptop. What is the MOST likely outcome under HIPAA?",c:["Notification to HHS only, since patient notification is required only above 100,000 affected records","Only an internal incident report, since breaches under 50,000 records fall below the reporting threshold","Breach notifications, OCR fines and a corrective action plan, since the exposed key defeats the encryption on the laptop","No breach notification, since encrypted ePHI is covered by the safe harbor even when the key was also taken"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. HIPAA requires notifying affected individuals as well as HHS (and media above 500 residents of a state). There is no 100,000-record cutoff for patient notice.","Incorrect. HIPAA has no 50,000-record floor. A breach of unsecured ePHI affecting 500 or more people triggers notices to individuals, HHS and, in some cases, the media.","Correct. Because the key was exposed, the data is treated as unsecured. HIPAA then requires breach notifications and exposes the provider to OCR penalties and a corrective action plan.","Incorrect. The encryption safe harbor applies only when the keys remain secure. Stolen keys mean the data is effectively unencrypted. It would apply if the key had been protected."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"Which statement about internal versus external compliance reporting is MOST accurate?",c:["Internal reports inform management and the board; external reports serve regulators and the public","Internal reporting satisfies regulators and customers; external reporting informs management and the board","Internal reporting is mandated by law in every jurisdiction; external reporting is always voluntary","Internal reporting covers only technical findings; external reporting covers only financial findings"],a:0,e:["Correct. Internal reports support management and board decisions, while external reports meet the needs of regulators, customers, auditors or the public.","Incorrect. This reverses the audiences. Regulators and customers receive external reports, and management receives internal ones.","Incorrect. Many external reports, such as breach notifications and regulatory filings, are legally required, and internal reporting requirements vary. Neither is uniformly mandatory or voluntary.","Incorrect. Both kinds of reports can cover technical, financial and operational matters. The difference is the audience, not the subject area."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"A privacy law grants individuals the right to receive their data in a structured, machine-readable format so they can move it to another provider. Which right is this?",c:["Right of access (obtain a copy of the data held about them)","Right to erasure (have the data deleted)","Right to rectification (correct inaccurate data)","Right to data portability (reuse data across services)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Access gives individuals a copy of their data and information about processing, but not necessarily in a format designed for transfer. It fits a request to see what is held.","Incorrect. Erasure lets individuals request deletion of their data. It does not involve transferring it to another provider.","Incorrect. Rectification corrects inaccurate data. It does not provide a portable copy.","Correct. Data portability gives individuals their data in a structured, commonly used, machine-readable format so they can transmit it to another controller."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"A company collects birthdates at newsletter sign-up 'just in case', although the newsletter only needs an email address. Which privacy principle is violated?",c:["Purpose limitation (using data for the stated purpose)","Data minimization (collecting only what is necessary)","Storage limitation (keeping data only as long as needed)","Data sovereignty (keeping data under local jurisdiction)"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Purpose limitation concerns using data for compatible purposes after collection. The issue here is collecting more fields than the newsletter needs.","Correct. Data minimization requires collecting only the data that is adequate and necessary for the stated purpose. Birthdates are not needed for a newsletter.","Incorrect. Storage limitation concerns how long data is retained. The stem concerns what is collected at sign-up.","Incorrect. Sovereignty concerns which jurisdiction's laws apply based on where data is stored. Nothing in the scenario involves location."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"An online retailer sells only within one US state and stores its data locally. Congress passes a national consumer privacy law. Which legal scope applies to the retailer?",c:["Only state law, because the retailer sells and stores data inside a single state","Only local ordinances, because the data is stored on local servers","National law, alongside any state and local laws that it does not preempt","Global regulations, as soon as any national privacy law passes"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A national law applies to businesses in the country regardless of where in the nation they operate. The retailer's single-state footprint does not exempt it.","Incorrect. Local ordinances do not displace national law, and where data is stored does not remove obligations under it.","Correct. A national law applies across the country and layers on top of state and local requirements that it does not preempt.","Incorrect. A national law does not create global regulation. Foreign laws apply only if the retailer handles data of people covered by them."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"Which is the BEST evidence that employees acknowledged the acceptable use policy?",c:["A manager's verbal confirmation that the team was briefed at a staff meeting","A signed or electronic attestation showing the employee, date and policy version","A copy of the policy posted on the intranet with a page-view counter showing monthly visits","A delivery receipt showing the policy email reached each employee's mailbox"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A verbal confirmation is not documented evidence of each person's acknowledgment and cannot be audited.","Correct. A recorded attestation identifies who acknowledged which version of the policy and when, which is verifiable evidence for auditors.","Incorrect. Page views show visits, not that a particular employee read and accepted the policy.","Incorrect. A delivery receipt shows the message arrived, not that the employee read or agreed to it."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"A company fails its PCI DSS requirements and its acquiring bank imposes monthly fines and higher transaction fees. Which category of consequence is this?",c:["Regulatory fines imposed by a government agency","Criminal penalties imposed by a court after prosecution","Civil liability from lawsuits by affected cardholders","Contractual sanctions through the merchant agreement"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. PCI DSS is an industry standard enforced by the card brands and acquirers, not a government regulator. Government fines would apply under laws such as state breach statutes.","Incorrect. Criminal penalties require a prosecution under criminal law. Fines from an acquiring bank are not criminal.","Incorrect. Cardholder lawsuits are civil actions. The stem describes penalties imposed directly by the acquirer.","Correct. PCI DSS obligations come from the merchant agreement, so fines and higher fees from the acquiring bank are contractual sanctions."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"Under GDPR, which party holds the right to request correction or deletion of personal data?",c:["The data subject (the individual)","The data controller (decides purposes)","The data processor (acts on instructions)","The data protection officer (DPO)"],a:0,e:["Correct. Data subjects, the individuals the data is about, hold the rights of rectification and erasure.","Incorrect. The controller must honor such requests but does not hold the right. It determines the purposes and means of processing.","Incorrect. The processor acts on the controller's behalf and helps it fulfil requests. It does not hold the right.","Incorrect. The DPO advises and monitors compliance and is a contact point for data subjects, but the rights belong to the individuals themselves."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"A compliance automation tool flags that 12 servers lack the logging required by the security standard. No compensating controls exist. What is the MOST appropriate next step?",c:["Notify the system owners by email and close the finding once they acknowledge receipt","Move the 12 servers to a separate network segment and remove them from the compliance report","Remediate the logging gap, track it to closure and retain evidence for auditors","Re-run the scan monthly until the findings clear on their own"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Acknowledging a notice does not fix the gap. A finding should close only after the logging is verified.","Incorrect. Segmentation may reduce exposure but removing the servers from the report hides the non-compliance without fixing it.","Correct. The required logging is missing, so the gap must be remediated and tracked to closure with documented evidence for auditors.","Incorrect. Findings do not resolve themselves. Waiting leaves the servers non-compliant and unmonitored."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"Which option BEST describes 'due care' in a compliance program?",c:["Investigating a vendor's financial and security posture before signing a contract","Verifying a third party's compliance certificates and audit reports at contract renewal","Maintaining a documented backup schedule with quarterly restore tests and offsite copies","Doing what a reasonable organization would do to protect assets and meet obligations"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Investigating before committing is due diligence, which is the research that comes before acting.","Incorrect. Verifying certificates is part of due diligence on a third party, not the continuing duty of reasonable protection.","Incorrect. A backup schedule is a single control that may be part of due care, but it is not the definition.","Correct. Due care is the ongoing practice of acting as a reasonable, prudent organization would to protect assets and meet legal and contractual obligations."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"A company holds customer personal data it no longer needs, well past its retention period. What is the compliance risk?",c:["Breaching availability requirements, since older records slow queries and backups","Breaching sovereignty requirements, since records past retention are replicated across regions","Violating retention and minimization requirements while increasing the impact of a breach","Breaching portability requirements, since expired records can no longer be exported"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Availability requirements concern access to data when needed. Excess data may affect performance but is not the compliance issue.","Incorrect. Sovereignty concerns the location of data and the jurisdiction that governs it. Retention of unneeded data does not relate to replication across regions.","Correct. Keeping data past its retention period violates retention and data minimization requirements and enlarges the amount of data exposed in a breach.","Incorrect. Portability concerns giving individuals a copy of their data. Having extra records does not impair that right."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"Under GDPR, how quickly must a controller typically notify the supervisory authority of a personal data breach that is likely to risk individuals' rights and freedoms?",c:["Within 24 hours of the breach occurring","Within 72 hours of becoming aware of it","Within 7 days of the investigation concluding","Within 30 days of notifying the affected data subjects"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. GDPR does not require notification within 24 hours. The clock also starts when the controller becomes aware, not when the breach occurs.","Correct. GDPR Article 33 requires notification to the supervisory authority without undue delay and, where feasible, within 72 hours of becoming aware of the breach.","Incorrect. The deadline does not wait for the investigation to conclude. Initial notification can be made in phases if details are incomplete.","Incorrect. The authority is notified within 72 hours. Data subject notification, when needed, is not a precondition and does not set a 30-day clock."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"Which consequence of non-compliance MOST directly affects a company's ability to continue operating a regulated line of business?",c:["Revocation of the operating license or authorization","Regulatory fines assessed for each violation found in the examination","Reputational damage and loss of customer trust","Contractual penalties under customer agreements"],a:0,e:["Correct. Regulators can revoke the license or authorization needed to operate a regulated business line, directly ending the ability to continue.","Incorrect. Fines are financial penalties that hurt but usually do not stop operations by themselves.","Incorrect. Reputation damage reduces business but does not remove legal permission to operate.","Incorrect. Contractual penalties are payments owed to customers. They do not remove the legal authority to operate."]},
{o:"5.4",q:"A privacy notice states that customer data will be used for order fulfillment. Marketing later uses the same data for targeted advertising without obtaining new consent. Which principle is violated?",c:["Data minimization (collecting only what is necessary)","Purpose limitation (using data for the stated purpose)","Storage limitation (keeping data only as long as needed)","Accuracy (keeping data correct and current)"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Minimization concerns how much data is collected. Here the data was collected appropriately, but it was reused.","Correct. Using data for a purpose beyond what was stated, such as advertising instead of order fulfillment, breaches purpose limitation unless there is a new legal basis such as consent.","Incorrect. Storage limitation concerns retention periods. The stem concerns reuse, not how long the data is held.","Incorrect. Accuracy concerns whether the data is correct. The data's correctness is not in question."]},

// ---------- 5.5 (14) ----------
{o:"5.5",q:"A penetration tester is given only the company name and must not alert the SOC; no one in the SOC is informed of the test. Which description BEST fits the engagement?",c:["Unknown-environment test with the SOC briefed in advance (overt)","Unknown-environment (black-box) test with an unannounced SOC (covert)","Partially known-environment test run as a purple-team exercise with the SOC","Known-environment (white-box) test with full documentation and an announced SOC"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. The tester's knowledge matches, but briefing the SOC makes the test overt. The stem says the SOC is not informed, so it is covert. Overt testing fits when the goal is validating tools with defender cooperation.","Correct. The tester has no inside knowledge (unknown environment) and the defenders are not told (covert), so the test measures realistic detection and response.","Incorrect. A purple-team exercise is collaborative, with attackers and defenders sharing information in real time. Here the SOC is deliberately kept unaware.","Incorrect. White-box testing gives the tester full documentation, and an announced SOC is overt. The tester here has only the company name."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"Which reconnaissance activity is PASSIVE?",c:["Running an Nmap SYN scan against the target's public IP address range","Banner grabbing the target's mail server with netcat on port 25","Sending a test phishing email to a published contact address","Searching certificate transparency logs for the target's registered subdomains"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A SYN scan sends packets to the target, which is active reconnaissance that can be logged and detected.","Incorrect. Banner grabbing connects to a service on the target, making it active reconnaissance.","Incorrect. Sending an email to the target is active engagement and creates records on the target's side.","Correct. Certificate transparency logs are public records, so searching them reveals subdomains without any direct contact with the target."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"A company's internal audit team reports to the CIO, whose systems they audit. What is the issue?",c:["Independence is impaired; internal audit should report to the audit committee or board","No issue, since reporting to the CIO gives auditors the technical access they need","Independence is impaired; internal audit should report to the CISO who owns the security controls under review","Objectivity is impaired; internal audit should be replaced by external auditors for every engagement"],a:0,e:["Correct. Auditors must be independent of the functions they audit, so internal audit should report to the audit committee or board, not to the CIO whose systems it reviews.","Incorrect. Technical access can be granted without a reporting line. Reporting to the audited executive creates a conflict that threatens independence.","Incorrect. The CISO owns controls under review, so reporting there would repeat the independence problem.","Incorrect. External audit complements internal audit but does not replace it for every engagement. The fix is a reporting line that preserves independence."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"Which assessment provides the MOST independent assurance to customers about a vendor's controls?",c:["An internal audit report reviewed by the audit committee","A vendor-completed CAIQ questionnaire","A management self-assessment signed by the CISO","A SOC 2 Type II report from an independent CPA firm"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Internal audit can be fairly objective but remains part of the organization, so it is less independent than an external auditor.","Incorrect. A questionnaire is self-reported by the vendor and offers little independence.","Incorrect. A management self-assessment is performed by the people responsible for the controls and is not independent.","Correct. An external CPA firm tests the controls independently and issues an opinion on their operating effectiveness, giving customers the strongest independent assurance."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"During a penetration test, the testers use their foothold on a compromised web server, with the same account privileges, to reach an internal database server. Which activity is this?",c:["Active reconnaissance of the internal subnet","Lateral movement from the web server","Privilege escalation on the web server host","Establishing persistence on the web server"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Active reconnaissance gathers information about targets before exploitation. The testers already have a foothold and are moving to a new host.","Correct. Using a compromised system as a pivot to reach other systems in the network is lateral movement in post-exploitation.","Incorrect. Privilege escalation raises the level of access on a system or account. The stem says the account privileges are unchanged.","Incorrect. Persistence maintains access across reboots or credential changes. The testers are reaching a new host, not keeping access."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"A regulator examines a bank and finds control deficiencies. What typically follows?",c:["Written findings with required corrective actions, deadlines and possible enforcement penalties","An automatic license renewal once the examiner's report is filed","A voluntary improvement plan with no deadlines unless a breach follows","Re-examination only after the bank requests it, with sanctions limited to public warnings"],a:0,e:["Correct. Regulatory examinations result in formal findings, required corrective actions with deadlines and potential enforcement actions or penalties.","Incorrect. Findings do not trigger automatic renewal. Deficiencies can put the license at risk.","Incorrect. Regulators typically set required remediation deadlines and do not wait for a breach.","Incorrect. Regulators schedule follow-up on their own timeline and can impose a range of sanctions including fines and restrictions."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"Which activity MOST clearly distinguishes a penetration test from a vulnerability scan?",c:["Enumerating hosts, ports and services within the agreed scope","Running automated tools against in-scope systems on a schedule","Producing a prioritized report with severity scores and fix guidance","Actively exploiting weaknesses to demonstrate real impact"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Enumeration is performed by both scans and penetration tests, so it does not distinguish them.","Incorrect. Vulnerability scans are run by automated tools, and pen testers use them too. Automation does not separate the two.","Incorrect. Both produce reports with severity scores. Reporting is not the differentiator.","Correct. A penetration test goes beyond identifying weaknesses by exploiting them, which proves real impact and reveals attack paths."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"Which option BEST describes an attestation of compliance (AOC) in the PCI DSS context?",c:["A report on compliance (ROC) detailing the assessor's test procedures and findings","A formal, signed declaration that the organization meets the standard","A self-assessment questionnaire (SAQ) with answers to each requirement","A quarterly vulnerability scan report from an approved scanning vendor (ASV)"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. The ROC is the detailed report of the assessor's testing, and the AOC accompanies it as a signed summary.","Correct. The AOC is a formal signed declaration of the organization's compliance status, submitted to acquirers or card brands.","Incorrect. An SAQ is a self-assessment tool that is completed before the AOC is signed. It is not the declaration itself.","Incorrect. An ASV scan report is quarterly evidence of external vulnerability scanning and is one input to compliance, not the declaration."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"A red team uses social engineering, physical intrusion and network attacks over several weeks to reach 'crown jewel' data. How does this differ from a standard penetration test?",c:["It is a scope-limited exploitation of a defined list of hosts, aiming to enumerate as many vulnerabilities as possible","It is a collaborative exercise in which attackers and defenders share techniques in real time","It is an objective-based adversary emulation that tests detection and response across people, process and technology","It is a scheduled automated scan of hosts that produces prioritized vulnerability lists"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. This describes a standard penetration test, which is bounded by scope and aims for breadth of findings. The red team here pursues a single objective across many attack vectors.","Incorrect. This describes a purple-team exercise, where red and blue cooperate. The red team here operates covertly against defenders to test their detection and response.","Correct. A red team emulates a real adversary pursuing an objective, using social, physical and technical attacks to test how well the organization detects and responds.","Incorrect. This describes a vulnerability scan, which identifies weaknesses without exploitation or human tactics such as social engineering."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"Before any technical activity begins in a penetration test engagement, which step must come FIRST?",c:["Planning and scoping with signed authorization and ROE","Passive reconnaissance of the target's public footprint","Vulnerability scanning of all in-scope hosts and services","Exploitation of externally facing services to gain initial access"],a:0,e:["Correct. Planning and scoping with written authorization and rules of engagement must precede any testing, since unauthorized testing may be illegal.","Incorrect. Passive reconnaissance is part of the technical work and follows the authorization and scoping step. It carries the least risk but still comes after planning.","Incorrect. Scanning touches target systems and requires prior authorization and scope definition.","Incorrect. Exploitation is a later phase and requires authorization first."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"An organization wants to test only its physical controls: door locks, badge readers, cameras and guard response. Which test type fits?",c:["Wireless penetration test","Social engineering assessment","Physical penetration test","Application penetration test"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A wireless test targets Wi-Fi and other radio networks. It would fit assessing access point security.","Incorrect. A social engineering assessment targets people through pretexting and phishing. It overlaps in tailgating, but the stem is about locks, readers, cameras and guards.","Correct. A physical penetration test attempts to defeat controls such as locks, badge readers, cameras and guards to gain unauthorized physical access.","Incorrect. An application test targets software vulnerabilities in web or mobile apps. It does not address physical barriers."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"With a limited budget and a short testing window, why might an organization prefer a known-environment (white-box) test over an unknown-environment (black-box) test?",c:["It most realistically simulates an outside attacker who has no prior knowledge of the target","It gives testers greater coverage and efficiency, finding more issues in limited time","It tests SOC detection more accurately because defenders are unaware of the activity","It lowers legal risk because written authorization is not required when documentation is provided"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Black-box testing, not white-box, simulates an outsider with no inside information. A white-box tester starts with extensive knowledge.","Correct. With documentation, credentials and architecture details, testers skip discovery and cover more of the environment in less time.","Incorrect. Defender awareness is determined by whether the test is covert or overt, not by tester knowledge. A white-box test can be run unannounced, but that is not its advantage.","Incorrect. Written authorization is required for every penetration test regardless of how much information the tester receives."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"Which deliverable should accompany a completed penetration test for executives?",c:["A technical appendix listing each exploit's payload, commands and raw output","A packet capture archive of all testing traffic with timestamps and source addresses","A tester timeline of tools used and hours spent on each testing phase","An executive summary with business risk ratings and prioritized remediation"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Payloads and raw output are for technical teams to reproduce and fix issues. Executives need summarized business risk.","Incorrect. Packet captures are low-level evidence that executives cannot easily interpret. They are for forensic or technical review.","Incorrect. A tool and hours timeline documents effort, not risk or recommended action. It may support invoicing.","Correct. Executives need a concise summary of risk in business terms, with prioritized remediation to guide decisions and funding."]},
{o:"5.5",q:"An organization uses an independent party to assess its controls against NIST SP 800-53 for a federal contract. What is this activity?",c:["Internal audit performed by the organization's own compliance team","Bug bounty program run through a crowdsourcing platform","Third-party assessment by an independent assessor organization (3PAO)","Attestation by the CISO that controls are implemented"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Internal audit is performed by the organization's own staff, which is not independent enough for an external requirement.","Incorrect. A bug bounty invites researchers to report vulnerabilities and does not assess controls against a framework.","Correct. An external, independent assessor evaluating controls against a standard like NIST 800-53 is a third-party assessment.","Incorrect. A CISO attestation is a management assertion, not an independent evaluation."]},

// ---------- 5.6 (12) ----------
{o:"5.6",q:"A phishing simulation program shows a 25% click rate in Q1 and 8% in Q3, but the report rate stayed at 5%. What should the awareness program focus on next?",c:["Make reporting easy with a one-click report button and rewards for users who report phishing","Escalate repeat clickers to HR and their managers after a second failed simulation","Replace simulations with annual compliance videos now that the click rate has improved","Measure only the click rate going forward, because it is the metric that improved"],a:0,e:["Correct. A flat report rate means users are not acting as sensors. Simplifying reporting and rewarding it builds the behavior that turns users into early detection.","Incorrect. Escalating clickers to HR targets punishment, which discourages reporting. Escalation might fit repeated willful policy violations, not a stagnant reporting rate.","Incorrect. The simulations are producing results, so dropping them for passive videos would reduce reinforcement. Videos fit baseline compliance training, not behavior change.","Incorrect. Tracking only clicks ignores the gap the stem highlights. Report rate is a key behavioral metric alongside click rate."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"An employee receives an urgent email from the 'CFO' requesting gift card purchases. What should awareness training teach as the FIRST action to confirm the request is genuine?",c:["Reply to the sender asking for the CFO's direct number to confirm the request is real","Buy one card as a test purchase and confirm the receipt with the sender by email","Verify the request with the CFO through a separate, known channel such as a phone call","Check the sender's display name and domain in the message header before deciding"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Replying reaches the attacker, who can supply a fake number and confirmation. Verification must go through a channel the attacker does not control.","Incorrect. A test purchase gives the attacker money and information, and the confirmation comes from the suspected attacker.","Correct. Out-of-band verification through a known, trusted channel defeats impersonation because the attacker cannot control that channel.","Incorrect. Header checks can reveal spoofing but are not definitive, since lookalike domains and compromised accounts can look legitimate. Out-of-band confirmation is more reliable."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"Which behavior is an indicator of an insider threat that awareness training should help employees recognize and report?",c:["A coworker working unusual hours but only on systems and data assigned to their role","A coworker accessing systems unrelated to their role and copying large data volumes after hours","A coworker repeatedly forgetting their password and requesting resets from the help desk each week","A coworker using a personal phone to approve MFA prompts for corporate logins"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Unusual hours alone are not suspicious when work stays within the person's role. It would warrant attention if combined with access beyond their duties.","Correct. Accessing resources unrelated to a person's job and moving large amounts of data after hours is a classic indicator of data theft by an insider.","Incorrect. Forgotten passwords are an ordinary support issue and do not by themselves indicate malicious intent.","Incorrect. Approving MFA on a personal phone is a common, approved practice in many organizations. It is not an indicator of insider activity."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"Which is the BEST way to measure the effectiveness of a security awareness program?",c:["The percentage of employees who completed the required annual training before the deadline","Employee satisfaction scores collected from post-training surveys and focus groups","The number of training modules published and updated during the year by the team","Behavior trends over time, such as phishing click rate, report rate and user-caused incidents"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Completion rates show participation, not whether behavior changed. They fit compliance tracking.","Incorrect. Satisfaction shows how people felt about the training, not whether it changed their security behavior.","Incorrect. The number of modules measures activity and output, not outcomes.","Correct. Behavioral metrics tied to training show whether employees are actually changing how they act and whether risk is falling."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"A company notices employees posting photos of their badges on social media. Which awareness training topic addresses this?",c:["Operational security (OPSEC) and social media use","Clean desk and document disposal practices for paper records","Anomalous behavior recognition and reporting procedures","Removable media handling and malware scanning rules"],a:0,e:["Correct. Posting badge photos leaks information attackers can use to clone badges or build pretexts. OPSEC and social media training teaches employees what not to share.","Incorrect. Clean desk and disposal practices address unattended documents and discarded paper. They would fit sensitive papers left on desks.","Incorrect. Recognizing anomalous behavior addresses insider or intruder activity. It does not cover what employees post publicly.","Incorrect. Removable media training addresses malware and data leakage from USB devices. It does not address badge photos."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"Staff work from home on company laptops. Which practice should awareness training emphasize MOST for protecting company data?",c:["Enabling split tunneling so personal traffic does not slow the corporate VPN","Using a personal cloud storage account as a backup of work files for resilience","Connecting through the company VPN and keeping the home router firmware updated","Using public Wi-Fi at coworking spaces and relying on the browser's HTTPS padlock for protection"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Split tunneling sends some traffic outside the corporate protections. It trades security for performance and is not what training should promote.","Incorrect. Copying work files to a personal cloud account moves company data outside organizational control and may violate policy.","Correct. A VPN protects traffic in transit, and a patched router with strong Wi-Fi encryption secures the home network, covering the core remote work risks.","Incorrect. The HTTPS padlock protects only individual sessions and does not protect against rogue access points or other local network attacks. A VPN would be needed."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"An employee accidentally emails a spreadsheet of customer SSNs to the wrong external address. What should awareness training teach them to do?",c:["Recall the message, then wait 24 hours to see whether the recipient replies before reporting","Report it immediately to security and privacy so the incident can be contained, assessed and escalated","Ask the recipient by email to delete it, and report only if they refuse","Document the details in a personal log and report it at the next scheduled security meeting"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Waiting delays containment and any required breach notification clocks. Recall attempts rarely work on external recipients.","Correct. Immediate reporting lets security and privacy teams contain the exposure, assess the impact and decide on notification duties.","Incorrect. A request to delete does not confirm deletion, and delaying the report undermines timely assessment.","Incorrect. A personal log does not start the response, and waiting for a scheduled meeting delays containment."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"Which approach to developing an awareness program MOST improves its relevance?",c:["Deliver one standard video to every employee so all staff receive identical, auditable content each year","Base the content on the most recent industry breach headlines, updated every month","Align the content to the regulation's required topic list, delivered once at hire for each new employee","Tailor content to roles and real risks, such as BEC for finance and secure coding for developers"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A single video is consistent and auditable but is not tailored to different job risks. It fits baseline compliance training.","Incorrect. Headlines may be interesting but do not necessarily match the risks employees face in their jobs.","Incorrect. Covering required topics once at hire satisfies compliance but does not keep content relevant over time or by role.","Correct. Role-based content addresses the threats each group actually faces, such as finance staff with BEC and developers with insecure code."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"A company runs quarterly phishing campaigns. One department always performs worst. What is the BEST response?",c:["Provide targeted just-in-time training and review process and workload factors in that department","Publish department rankings monthly to motivate improvement through peer comparison","Increase the frequency of simulations for that department only, with no added training","Exclude the department from simulations until the next annual training cycle"],a:0,e:["Correct. Targeted help and a look at root causes, such as workload or workflow, address why the department struggles without damaging culture.","Incorrect. Public rankings can shame staff and discourage reporting. A private, supportive approach is preferred.","Incorrect. More tests without more training only measures failure more often and does not teach better behavior.","Incorrect. Excluding the weakest department hides the highest risk and removes the reinforcement it needs."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"A traveler will view sensitive data on a laptop in a crowded airport lounge. Which practice BEST counters shoulder surfing?",c:["Enabling full-disk encryption and a pre-boot password on the laptop before travel","Connecting through a VPN with a kill switch rather than the airport Wi-Fi","Using a privacy screen filter and facing away from other travelers","Locking the screen automatically after five minutes of inactivity"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Full-disk encryption protects data if the device is lost or stolen, not what an onlooker can see on a screen in use.","Incorrect. A VPN protects traffic from network eavesdropping, not from people watching the display.","Correct. Privacy filters and careful positioning limit what bystanders can see, which directly counters shoulder surfing.","Incorrect. An inactivity lock protects an unattended device. It does not stop someone from watching while the screen is in use."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"Which password management practice should awareness training PROMOTE?",c:["Using a strong passphrase with a numeric suffix that changes for each site","Using the browser's built-in password saving, synced to a personal account","Using a personal spreadsheet file encrypted with a single master password","Using a company-approved password manager with unique passphrases and MFA"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A pattern with a changing suffix is predictable, and a compromise of one site can help guess others.","Incorrect. Syncing credentials to a personal account moves company secrets outside organizational control and relies on that account's security.","Incorrect. A homemade spreadsheet lacks the auditing, sharing controls and phishing-resistant autofill of a managed tool, and it depends on one master password.","Correct. A company-approved manager generates and stores unique credentials under organizational control, and MFA protects the vault."]},
{o:"5.6",q:"Which reporting cadence is typically used to keep executives informed of awareness program trends?",c:["Ad hoc reports issued only when a security incident involves user behavior","Annual reports summarizing training completion once per fiscal year","Real-time dashboards sent to executives with raw event counts each day","Recurring reports, such as quarterly, with trend metrics and actions"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Reporting only after incidents is reactive and gives no view of trends. It leaves executives uninformed until something goes wrong.","Incorrect. An annual report is too infrequent to guide timely changes and focuses on completion rather than behavior trends.","Incorrect. Daily raw counts overwhelm executives with detail and lack context or recommendations.","Correct. Regular summaries with trend metrics and actions give leadership the information to oversee and fund the program."]}
]);
